<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  RKMCinema — the app's real Info.plist.

  ⚠ TWO THINGS ABOUT THIS FILE, both learned on the first real build:

  1. POINT `INFOPLIST_FILE` AT IT — Build Settings → Info.plist File = `Config/Info.plist` — and set
     GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = NO. It is a file rather than INFOPLIST_KEY_* settings for one reason:
     **App Transport Security is a nested dictionary**, which the INFOPLIST_KEY_* settings cannot
     express (apple/WORKFLOW.md §2).
  2. ⚠ **IT MUST LIVE OUTSIDE `RKMCinema/`, the target's synchronized source folder.** A file inside
     that folder *is* a target member, so this plist was at once **copied into the app as a resource**
     and **processed as the Info.plist**, and the build died with
     `error: Multiple commands produce …RKMCinema.app/Info.plist`. `Config/` is referenced only by the
     build setting and by nothing in the project — which is exactly what keeps it out of that
     automatic membership. The tvOS app needs the same arrangement.

  Skip it and the app appears broken: the address is typed by a human and may be plain
  http:// to a bare LAN IP, which iOS blocks by default (docs/APPLE_CLIENTS_PLAN.md §2.2). The
  launch banner logs the resolved ATS values so a mis-pointed setting is visible immediately.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>RKMCinema</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<!-- An empty UILaunchScreen is the documented way to get the default launch screen; omit it
	     and the app is letterboxed on modern devices. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  ⚠ App Transport Security — READ BEFORE ADDING A SECOND KEY.

	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads is the ONLY key here, deliberately. The address is typed by a human at
	  runtime, so it can be ANY host — a raw LAN IP, a Tailscale name, a future domain — and no
	  build-time exception list can cover that.

	  ⚠⚠ DO NOT ADD NSAllowsLocalNetworking (nor NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent / …ForMedia)
	  ALONGSIDE IT. On iOS 10+ the presence of any of those more specific keys makes ATS **ignore
	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads entirely** — so with both keys set, plain http:// was blocked for every
	  host that is not "local networking". That is not theory: the first real connect in the app
	  failed as exactly this —

	      http://rkm-hp.tail8d5e8.ts.net:8124
	      NSURLErrorDomain -1022 — the resource could not be loaded because the App Transport
	      Security policy requires the use of a secure connection

	  — while the same plain http:// to a LAN IP still worked, because the local-networking
	  exemption covered that one. The narrower-looking key was the more damaging one.

	  (App Review asks for a justification for NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. Irrelevant unless this is
	  ever submitted to the store.)
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
